INFO  [Claude Code] ## Summary
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] I've resolved the issue preventing test creation for `cn.keking.service.OfficePluginManager.destroyOfficeManager()`. Here's what was done:
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] ### The Problem
INFO  [Claude Code] The test context initialization was failing because `OfficePluginManager` requires an actual LibreOffice/OpenOffice installation, which is not available in the test environment. When the `@PostConstruct` method `startOfficeManager()` runs, it calls `LocalOfficeUtils.getDefaultOfficeHome()` which returns null, causing a RuntimeException.
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] ### The Solution
INFO  [Claude Code] I created a Diffblue Cover configuration file at `/home/ubuntu/kkFileView/server/dcover.json` that tells Diffblue to use `cn.keking.DiffblueTestBase` as the custom base class for all generated tests.
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] **Key components that work together:**
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] 1. **dcover.json** (newly created at server/dcover.json:1-11) - Configures Diffblue Cover to use the custom base class
INFO  [Claude Code] 2. **DiffblueTestBase** (existing at server/src/test/java/cn/keking/DiffblueTestBase.java:1-18) - Base class that imports TestOfficeConfig
INFO  [Claude Code] 3. **TestOfficeConfig** (existing at server/src/test/java/cn/keking/config/TestOfficeConfig.java:1-28) - Provides a @Primary mocked OfficePluginManager bean that replaces the real implementation during tests
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] This approach ensures that when tests are generated, they'll extend `DiffblueTestBase`, which automatically imports the `TestOfficeConfig`. The mocked `OfficePluginManager` bean prevents the actual initialization code from running, avoiding the RuntimeException.
INFO  [Claude Code] 
INFO  [Claude Code] ### Next Steps
INFO  [Claude Code] As you mentioned, you may need to run `mvn install -DskipTests` if this change affects the classpath. The next time Diffblue Cover runs, it should use the custom base class and successfully generate tests for `OfficePluginManager` methods without encountering the initialization error.
